course: dictionary
lesson: 1754
title: Clauses: groups of words with a verb
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A clause is a group of words that has a verb in it, and usually a subject, the person or thing doing the action. «we went outside» is a clause: we is the subject and went is the verb. A phrase is a group of words without its own verb, like «the big red bus».
explain: Some clauses make sense on their own. «The dog barked.» is a main clause, because it is a whole sentence. «when the postman came» has a verb too, but it leans on a main clause and cannot stand alone. That kind is a subordinate clause.
explain: The same words can be a phrase or a clause. «after lunch» is only a phrase, because it has no verb. «after we had lunch» is a clause, because had is a verb. Ask yourself: does this group have a verb of its own?
fact: A clause is a group of words that has a verb and usually a subject.
fact: A main clause makes sense on its own, like «The dog barked.»
fact: A subordinate clause has a verb but does not make sense on its own, like «when the postman came».
fact: A relative clause tells you more about a noun, like «the boy who won».
fact: In a contract or law, a clause is one section or rule in a written agreement.
exercise: «when the bell rang» is a clause. What kind of clause is it on its own? | A main clause that makes sense alone | A phrase with no verb | A rule in a contract | *A subordinate clause, which needs a main clause
exercise: Which group of words is a clause? | *after we had lunch | the big red bus | after lunch | on the table
exercise: In «the boy who won», which part is a relative clause that tells you more about the boy? | a red ball | *who won | won | the boy
test: Which group is a main clause? | after lunch | *The dog barked. | when the postman came | the big dog
test: In a phone contract, what is a clause? | a funny story | a word for a noun | *one section or rule in a written agreement | a type of verb
exam: A pupil writes «when we got home» and says it is a full sentence. Is she right? | Yes, it has a verb, so it is a main clause | Yes, because it starts with when | No, it is only a phrase with no verb | *No, it has a verb but needs a main clause, so it is subordinate
